        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "LiveStrong Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/livestrong-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Who made LiveStrong treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Johnson Health Tech, a large Taiwanese manufacturer that also owns and builds Horizon Fitness, Matrix Fitness and Vision Fitness. LiveStrong was a licensing arrangement with the cancer foundation, not a manufacturer. This is the most useful fact for an owner: the company that built your machine is still trading and still making treadmills, so parts and cross-references exist.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why were LiveStrong treadmills discontinued?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The licensing partnership ended after the collapse of Lance Armstrong's reputation following the doping investigations, which made the brand name commercially untenable. Nothing was recalled and nothing was defective — the manufacturer simply continued under its other brands. The machines were generally well regarded and their discontinuation says nothing about their quality.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where can I get parts for a LiveStrong treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Start with Johnson Health Tech's service channels, treating it as a Johnson product rather than a LiveStrong one. Then cross-reference against Horizon and Vision machines of the same era, since the same manufacturer frequently shared components across those lines — verify by part number and dimensions. Common wear items like belts and rollers are generic. Free scrap LS machines on local listings make excellent donors for console boards.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a LiveStrong treadmill worth repairing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always if the fault is a belt, deck, roller, safety key or wiring break — those are cheap parts on a sound frame. Probably if it is the motor. Think hard if it is the upper console board, which is the hardest part to source. Not if there are several simultaneous faults. The deciding test: would you pay the repair cost for this machine in working order today, at fifteen years old with no warranty?",
          },
        },
