        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-happened" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. What Happened to LiveStrong Fitness</a>
          <a href="#the-models" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Models Still in Homes</a>
          <a href="#parts" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Getting Parts for a Discontinued Machine</a>
          <a href="#console-problem" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Console Problem</a>
          <a href="#worth-repairing" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Is It Worth Repairing?</a>
          <a href="#buying-used" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Buying a Used LiveStrong Today</a>
          <a href="#what-to-buy-instead" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. What to Buy Instead</a>
          <a href="#keeping-it-going" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Keeping One Going Indefinitely</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people searching for this brand are not shopping. They own one, something has gone
          wrong, and the manufacturer’s website no longer exists. That is a solvable problem, and
          the solution starts with the fact that LiveStrong never built a treadmill in the first
          place.
        </p>

        <div id="what-happened" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Happened to LiveStrong Fitness</h2>
<p>
            LiveStrong Fitness was a licensing arrangement rather than a manufacturer. The name
            belonged to the cancer foundation associated with Lance Armstrong; the machines were
            designed, built and supported by <strong>Johnson Health Tech</strong>, a large
            Taiwanese fitness equipment manufacturer that also owns and builds
            <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon Fitness</a>,
            <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix Fitness</a> and
            Vision Fitness.
          </p>
          <p>
            The treadmills sold through the mid-2000s and into the early 2010s, positioned in the
            mid-market — better built than the supermarket tier, cheaper than the commercial
            brands, and with a charitable association that was part of the appeal. They were, by
            general consensus, decent machines. The frames were solid, the decks were reasonable
            for the money, and they were not notably unreliable.
          </p>
          <p>
            The partnership ended following the collapse of Armstrong’s reputation after the
            doping investigations, and the fitness line was wound down. Johnson Health Tech
            continued exactly as before under its other brands. No machines were recalled, nothing
            was defective, and the product simply stopped existing because the name on it became
            commercially untenable.
          </p>
          <p>
            That history matters practically for one reason. An orphaned brand usually means
            orphaned hardware — a machine built by a company that no longer exists, with parts
            nobody stocks. That is not what happened here. The company that built these treadmills
            is large, healthy and still making treadmills on the same production lines under other
            names. Your machine is not an orphan. It is a Johnson Health Tech treadmill with a
            discontinued badge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Models Still in Homes</h2>
<p>
            The range used an LS prefix with a numeric designation, ascending roughly with
            capability and price — the LS8.0T, LS10.0T and LS13.0T are the ones that turn up most
            often, with the LS10.0T being by a distance the most common machine still in use.
            Higher numbers generally meant a longer deck, a larger motor, more incline and a better
            console.
          </p>
          <p>
            Specifications changed between model years within the same name, which is normal in
            this industry and is the reason the note at the top of this page matters. Two machines
            both badged LS10.0T can have different motor ratings, different deck dimensions and
            different console hardware depending on when they were built. If you are diagnosing a
            fault or ordering a part, the model and serial number on the frame is the only
            identifier that means anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            Broadly, these were mid-market home treadmills of their era: folding frames, motorised
            incline on most units, decks in the range you would expect for a home running machine
            of the time, and LED or basic LCD consoles with onboard programmes. No subscriptions,
            no accounts, no cloud services to be switched off — which, fifteen years later, has
            turned out to be an advantage. A LiveStrong console does today exactly what it did when
            it was new, which is not true of every connected treadmill sold since.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weight capacities and deck lengths were adequate rather than generous by current
            standards. If you are assessing whether to keep one, compare it honestly against a
            modern machine using our
            <a href="/treadmill-buyers-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buyer’s guide</a>
            rather than against what it cost new.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Getting Parts for a Discontinued Machine</h2>
<p>
            This is the section most readers came for, and the news is better than expected.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Start with Johnson Health Tech’s support channels.</strong> The manufacturer
            supported these machines after the brand ended, and its service operation is the
            single best source of a correct part number for a given serial. Approach it as a
            Johnson product rather than as a LiveStrong one — that framing alone gets a more useful
            answer than searching a dead brand name.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cross-reference against Horizon and Vision machines of the same era.</strong>
            This is the key practical insight. Because the same manufacturer built all of these
            lines in the same period, components are frequently shared or closely related across
            them — rollers, motors, controller boards, incline motors and belts. A part that is
            unlisted under a LiveStrong number may be readily available under a Horizon one. Always
            verify by part number and physical dimensions rather than assuming, but check, because
            the hit rate is high.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Generic parts cover most of what actually fails.</strong> The reassuring reality
            of treadmill repair is that the common wear items are not brand-specific in any deep
            sense. Belts are sized by width and circumference and are widely available cut to size.
            Rollers, deck boards, motor brushes, drive belts and power switches are commodity
            items. Silicone lubricant is silicone lubricant. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            covers what each of these typically runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Salvage is a genuine strategy here.</strong> These machines are given away
            constantly — free or nearly free on local listings, usually because a console has died
            or nobody wants to move one. Buying a scrap LS unit for the cost of collection gives you
            a complete donor for exactly the parts that are hardest to source. If you intend to keep
            yours long-term, a donor machine in the garage is worth more than a box of spares.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Manuals are findable.</strong> Archived copies of the original PDFs circulate on
            manual repositories and fitness forums. Search the exact model designation rather than
            the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Console Problem</h2>
<p>
            If a LiveStrong machine has a terminal fault, it is disproportionately likely to be
            electronic rather than mechanical, and there is a reason for that beyond bad luck.
          </p>
          <p>
            Frames, motors and rollers are simple, robust and repairable. Console boards and
            controller boards are neither — they are model-specific, they are the hardest thing to
            source for a discontinued line, and they are the component where a fifteen-year-old
            machine genuinely does hit a wall. Capacitors age, membrane switches wear through,
            displays fail.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before concluding a board is dead, rule out the cheap causes, because they account for
            a large share of apparent console failures. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">power fault guide</a>
            walks the full sequence, but in summary: check the safety key and its magnet, which
            wear and are a common and trivially fixable cause of a dead console; check the wiring
            loom at the pivot point where a folding deck flexes the cable thousands of times, which
            is where breaks occur; check the reed switch and the circuit breaker on the frame.
            Several of those are five-minute fixes mistaken for dead electronics every day.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the board really has failed, the options narrow to a used board from a donor
            machine, a cross-compatible board from a Johnson-built equivalent, or a board-level
            repair by a specialist. A generic universal motor controller is sometimes viable for a
            dead lower board, though it usually costs you the incline function and the programmes.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is also the moment the economics get decided, which is the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-repairing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Is It Worth Repairing?</h2>
<p>
            A clear framework, because sentiment about a machine you have owned for a decade tends
            to distort this.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Almost always repair</strong> if the fault is a belt, a deck, a roller, a
            drive belt, a safety key, a wiring break or anything requiring lubrication and
            adjustment. These are inexpensive parts on a frame that is fundamentally sound, and the
            work is within reach of a competent owner. A new belt and a proper service transforms
            how one of these machines feels, and people routinely conclude their treadmill is worn
            out when it is simply dry — our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            is the first thing to try on any machine that feels tired.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Probably repair</strong> if it is a motor, provided the rest of the machine is
            in good condition and you can source one at a sensible price. A motor on a sound frame
            is a reasonable investment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Think hard</strong> if it is an upper console board, because this is where cost
            and difficulty converge and where sourcing is genuinely uncertain.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Probably do not repair</strong> if the frame is damaged, if the deck and belt
            are worn out at the same time as an electronic fault, or if the machine has been run dry
            for years. A machine with several simultaneous faults is telling you it has reached the
            end, and our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan guide</a>
            covers how to read that pattern.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The test that settles it:</strong> would you pay the repair cost for this
            machine in working order today, knowing it is fifteen years old with no warranty? If
            the answer is no, the repair is not worth doing regardless of how reasonable the part
            price looks in isolation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Buying a Used LiveStrong Today</h2>
<p>
            They come up constantly, they are cheap, and they are often free. Whether that is an
            opportunity depends entirely on what you want it for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The case for:</strong> a solid mid-market frame from a reputable manufacturer,
            a console that needs no account and will never be switched off remotely, and a price
            that is frequently nothing at all. For a walker, or for someone who wants a machine in
            a garage without spending money, a working LS10.0T collected for free is difficult to
            argue with.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The case against:</strong> no warranty, uncertain maintenance history,
            electronics that are at the age where they fail, and a deck that is probably at or past
            its service life. You are acquiring a machine that owes you nothing and could need work
            immediately.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How to inspect one</strong> follows our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standard checklist</a>,
            with particular weight on three things. Run every console function including incline,
            because that is the expensive failure. Slide a hand between belt and deck — dry or
            gritty means it ran unlubricated for years, and the motor has already spent much of its
            life. And walk on it at speed to check the belt does not hesitate under load.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price it at scrap value plus the cost of a service.</strong> Budget for a belt,
            lubricant and a few hours immediately. If the asking price plus that work exceeds what a
            known-good modern budget machine costs, the maths has stopped working —
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the under-$500 field</a>
            is the relevant comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And do not pay a premium for the badge.</strong> Occasionally these are listed
            optimistically as collectible. They are not. It is a Johnson Health Tech treadmill with
            a discontinued sticker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Buy Instead</h2>
<p>
            When a LiveStrong finally goes, the natural replacement is closer than most owners
            realise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Horizon is the direct successor</strong> in everything but name. Same
            manufacturer, same design philosophy, same mid-market position, same no-subscription
            approach to consoles. If you liked your LiveStrong, you will recognise a
            <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon</a> immediately —
            the <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T101</a> is
            the closest spiritual replacement for an entry LS unit, and the
            <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">7.0 AT</a> steps
            up for anyone doing interval work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Matrix is the same company at the commercial end</strong>, if the replacement
            budget has grown since the original purchase.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole is the obvious cross-shop</strong> for anyone who valued the plainness.
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> builds heavy
            machines with long warranties and no account requirement, which is close to what
            LiveStrong was doing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Avoid the subscription trap on the way out.</strong> The thing a fifteen-year-old
            LiveStrong does best is work without asking permission. A modern machine whose console
            is gated behind a membership does not have that property, and it is worth knowing that
            before you replace like for like —
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our no-subscription roundup</a>
            covers which current machines still behave the way yours does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="keeping-it-going" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Keeping One Going Indefinitely</h2>
<p>
            If the plan is to keep it rather than replace it, a discontinued machine rewards a
            slightly different maintenance posture than a current one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lubricate on schedule and do not skip it.</strong> On a machine whose motor and
            controller are effectively irreplaceable, friction is the thing that will finish it.
            This is the highest-value habit available to you, and our
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            covers how often.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the motor compartment clean.</strong> Dust insulates, heat kills
            electronics, and a fifteen-year-old board has less margin than a new one. Our
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vacuuming guide</a>
            covers the method safely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the wear parts before you need them.</strong> Belts and rollers are
            available now. If this machine is your long-term plan, a spare belt on a shelf is
            cheap insurance against a supply chain that is only going to get thinner.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Take the free donor machine when it appears.</strong> Somebody near you is
            giving away an LS unit with a dead deck and a perfectly good console, and that console
            is the part you cannot buy. This single habit is what separates owners who keep these
            machines running for twenty years from owners who do not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treat belt tension and tracking as routine.</strong> Both drift with age and
            both are adjustable —
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring</a>
            and
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tensioning</a>
            take minutes and prevent wear that is expensive to reverse.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who made LiveStrong treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Johnson Health Tech, a large Taiwanese manufacturer that also owns and builds Horizon Fitness, Matrix Fitness and Vision Fitness. LiveStrong was a licensing arrangement with the cancer foundation, not a manufacturer. This is the most useful fact for an owner: the company that built your machine is still trading and still making treadmills, so parts and cross-references exist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why were LiveStrong treadmills discontinued?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The licensing partnership ended after the collapse of Lance Armstrong's reputation following the doping investigations, which made the brand name commercially untenable. Nothing was recalled and nothing was defective — the manufacturer simply continued under its other brands. The machines were generally well regarded and their discontinuation says nothing about their quality.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I get parts for a LiveStrong treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with Johnson Health Tech's service channels, treating it as a Johnson product rather than a LiveStrong one. Then cross-reference against Horizon and Vision machines of the same era, since the same manufacturer frequently shared components across those lines — verify by part number and dimensions. Common wear items like belts and rollers are generic. Free scrap LS machines on local listings make excellent donors for console boards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a LiveStrong treadmill worth repairing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always if the fault is a belt, deck, roller, safety key or wiring break — those are cheap parts on a sound frame. Probably if it is the motor. Think hard if it is the upper console board, which is the hardest part to source. Not if there are several simultaneous faults. The deciding test: would you pay the repair cost for this machine in working order today, at fifteen years old with no warranty?
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the modern equivalent of a LiveStrong treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon Fitness, which is the same manufacturer, the same mid-market position and the same subscription-free approach to consoles. The T101 is the closest replacement for an entry-level LS unit and the 7.0 AT steps up for interval training. Matrix is the same company at the commercial end, and Sole is the obvious cross-shop for anyone who valued the plainness.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a LiveStrong treadmill need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, and that is one of its quiet advantages in 2026. These consoles are entirely self-contained — no account, no app, no cloud service that can be discontinued. A fifteen-year-old LiveStrong does today exactly what it did when new, which is not true of every connected treadmill sold since. Worth remembering when you replace it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes with a Discontinued Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Three of these four end with a working machine being scrapped, which is the outcome this page exists to prevent.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the brand being gone means parts are gone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">This is the error that scraps repairable machines. The manufacturer never went anywhere — Johnson Health Tech still builds treadmills under Horizon and Matrix, components were frequently shared across those lines in the same period, and the common wear items were never brand-specific at all. Search by part number and by the Johnson equivalent, not by a dead brand name.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Condemning the console before checking the safety key</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A dead display is assumed to be a failed board far more often than it is one. Safety keys and their magnets weaken with age, and the wiring loom at a folding deck’s pivot flexes thousands of times and breaks inside the insulation. Both are cheap, both present exactly like a dead console, and both are ruled out in ten minutes before anyone spends money.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Passing up the free donor machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Identical machines are given away constantly because a console died or nobody wanted to move one. That machine contains the exact parts you cannot buy — boards, incline motors, consoles — for the price of collection. Owners who keep these treadmills running for twenty years are almost always the ones who took the donor when it appeared.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping lubrication on an irreplaceable motor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">On a current machine, running dry costs you a motor you can replace. On a discontinued one it costs you the machine, because friction rises, the controller works harder, and the controller is the part with no supply chain behind it. This is the single highest-value habit available to a LiveStrong owner and it takes ten minutes every few months.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

